<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml" xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib" xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml" xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">
	<!-- Named arguments are a deliberate prose-parameter documentation convention (GRAMMAR §10:
	     from:, to:, description:). Protecting them takes TWO settings, because inspect and cleanup
	     read different axes and do not share a switch.

	     1. The inspection severities below stop the IDE and inspectcode REPORTING a deliberate name
	        as removable. They have no effect on cleanup: cleanup overrides the severity of every
	        rule its enabled modules need, so DO_NOT_SHOW cannot reach it.
	     2. The cleanup profile below stops cleanup REWRITING it. "Arrange argument style" is the
	        cleanup task that normalises arguments to positional, and turning it off is the only way
	        to leave argument style as-authored — the style setting itself is binary (positional or
	        named) with no "leave alone" value.

	     Contributors need not run ReSharper; the file is simply ignored by anything that doesn't. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ArgumentsStyleLiteral/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ArgumentsStyleStringLiteral/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ArgumentsStyleNamedExpression/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ArgumentsStyleOther/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ArgumentsStyleAnonymousFunction/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

	<!-- The cleanup profile: "Built-in: Full Cleanup" with one task off, ArrangeArgumentsStyle.

	     A profile is a single XML-encoded blob under /Default/CodeStyle/CodeCleanup/Profiles/=<Name>/,
	     and it is NOT a diff against a built-in: any task the blob omits reads back as the
	     descriptor's default, which is off. So this enumerates every C# cleanup task Full Cleanup
	     turns on. The list was read off ReSharper 2026.1.2's own cleanup modules, not guessed —
	     re-derive it on a ReSharper upgrade (CodeStylesCleanupModule.ALL_CODE_STYLES and the
	     SetDefaultSetting(FULL) arm of each ICodeCleanupModule). A task JetBrains adds later will
	     be missing here and silently not run; a missing task is a nicety not applied, never a
	     rewrite of code you meant to keep.

	     Three tasks are False deliberately, and each one is required:

	     - ArrangeArgumentsStyle, per the named-argument note above.
	     - CSReorderTypeMembers, because reordering is the only task that moves a member wholesale
	       across a file, and several files here pin line numbers. SpecValidationSpecs.cs is the
	       sharp case: forty expected messages in SpecValidationTests.cs quote its anchors as
	       literal "SpecValidationSpecs.cs:N" text, so one hoisted member silently moves forty
	       assertions. Member order is a nicety; a line-pinned golden is not. Leaving it off is what
	       lets any file in the repo be passed to cleanup without first consulting a list.
	     - CSUpdateFileHeader, which Full Cleanup leaves off too.

	     The trailing newline at EOF is NOT settable here — insert_final_newline is an .editorconfig
	     standard property with no .DotSettings equivalent. The repo carries a two-line .editorconfig
	     for exactly that one knob, and that is where to change it. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/CodeCleanup/Profiles/=SlideRule_003A_0020Full_0020Cleanup_0020_0028keep_0020named_0020arguments_0029/@EntryIndexedValue">&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-16"?&gt;&lt;Profile name="SlideRule: Full Cleanup (keep named arguments)"&gt;&lt;CSReformatCode&gt;True&lt;/CSReformatCode&gt;&lt;CSharpFormatDocComments&gt;True&lt;/CSharpFormatDocComments&gt;&lt;CSharpReformatComments&gt;True&lt;/CSharpReformatComments&gt;&lt;CSOptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;OptimizeUsings&gt;True&lt;/OptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;EmbraceInRegion&gt;False&lt;/EmbraceInRegion&gt;&lt;RegionName&gt;&lt;/RegionName&gt;&lt;/CSOptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;CSShortenReferences&gt;True&lt;/CSShortenReferences&gt;&lt;CSReorderTypeMembers&gt;False&lt;/CSReorderTypeMembers&gt;&lt;CSUpdateFileHeader&gt;False&lt;/CSUpdateFileHeader&gt;&lt;RemoveCodeRedundancies&gt;True&lt;/RemoveCodeRedundancies&gt;&lt;CSUseAutoProperty&gt;True&lt;/CSUseAutoProperty&gt;&lt;CSMakeFieldReadonly&gt;True&lt;/CSMakeFieldReadonly&gt;&lt;CSMakeAutoPropertyGetOnly&gt;True&lt;/CSMakeAutoPropertyGetOnly&gt;&lt;CSArrangeQualifiers&gt;True&lt;/CSArrangeQualifiers&gt;&lt;CSFixBuiltinTypeReferences&gt;True&lt;/CSFixBuiltinTypeReferences&gt;&lt;CSCodeStyleAttributes ArrangeVarStyle="True" ArrangeTypeAccessModifier="True" ArrangeTypeMemberAccessModifier="True" SortModifiers="True" ArrangeAccessors="True" ArrangeArgumentsStyle="False" RemoveRedundantParentheses="True" AddMissingParentheses="True" ArrangeBraces="True" ArrangeAttributes="True" ArrangeCodeBodyStyle="True" ArrangeTrailingCommas="True" ArrangeObjectCreation="True" ArrangeDefaultValue="True" ArrangeEmptyString="True" ArrangeNamespaces="True" ArrangeNullCheckingPattern="True" /&gt;&lt;/Profile&gt;</s:String>

	<!-- ReSharper's "profile to use when nobody picks one".

	     A runner that honours this key reads it before cleaning, so a cleanup call naming no
	     profile still gets the profile above and the deliberate named arguments survive. Omitting
	     the profile argument is the intended path there; naming it is merely harmless.

	     Bare `jb cleanupcode` never reads this key: jb always defaults to Full Cleanup, so a direct
	     CLI run must still pass the profile by name. jb's switch for that is not spelled literally
	     here: XML forbids adjacent hyphens inside a comment, and one such spelling once left this
	     file unreadable to every conformant XML parser while jb itself carried on regardless. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/CodeCleanup/SilentCleanupProfile/@EntryValue">SlideRule: Full Cleanup (keep named arguments)</s:String>

	<!-- 'var' only where the initializer makes the type evident; explicit type everywhere else.

	     One policy, three category keys, all pinned to the same value. Until 2026-08-14 two of
	     them (built-in and simple types) were supplied by this machine's personal global layer
	     (GlobalSettingsStorage.DotSettings), which jb reads beneath this file: the prevailing
	     style (explicit string/XDocument for method-call initializers, var for literals and new
	     expressions) was held up by a file the repo does not carry. The third, ForOtherTypes,
	     stood at its factory default of always-var, which is why generic-returning calls kept
	     var even where nothing on the line names the type (var selected = SelectRules(...)).
	     Pinning all three here makes the policy the repo's own on any machine and closes the
	     ForOtherTypes gap. Cleanup enforces it through the profile's ArrangeVarStyle task, in
	     both directions, on every file it touches. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/CSharpVarKeywordUsage/ForBuiltInTypes/@EntryValue">UseVarWhenEvident</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/CSharpVarKeywordUsage/ForSimpleTypes/@EntryValue">UseVarWhenEvident</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/CSharpVarKeywordUsage/ForOtherTypes/@EntryValue">UseVarWhenEvident</s:String>

	<!-- The severities that make the policy above enforceable rather than merely declared.

	     The three style entries say what the style IS; on their own they only take effect when
	     something runs cleanup. These four say a departure is visible without one — inspectcode
	     reports it, so the zero-at-Warning baseline fails on the drift instead of absorbing it.
	     Suggestion, ReSharper's default here, is not enough: the baseline is read at Warning, and
	     a suggestion sits below it silently.

	     Inspect-only, like the DO_NOT_SHOW entries at the top of this file and for the same
	     reason: cleanup overrides the severity of every rule its enabled modules need, so nothing
	     set here reaches ArrangeVarStyle in either direction.

	     DeconstructionDeclarations is promoted alongside the other three even though no
	     ForDeconstructionDeclarations style key sits above it — the declarations in the tree are
	     already explicit and inspect reports none, so the promotion holds the line where it is.
	     Should that ever red, pin the missing style key rather than demoting this one.

	     The _005F in each key is the escape for the underscore in the rule id, and it is the whole
	     entry: an underscore is how this format introduces an escape, so it cannot also stand for
	     itself. These four carried the literal spelling from the day they were written until
	     2026-08-20, and in that form they did nothing at all — the entry parses, the file loads, the
	     rule keeps its default of hint, and a drifted declaration sits below the level the baseline
	     reads, which is the exact state the entries were added to escape. Nothing reported it,
	     because a no-op key and a working one are indistinguishable until a violation exists to
	     report. What settled it: with the literal spelling the rule stayed at hint even when the
	     value was raised to ERROR, while a control rule promoted from hint in the same file moved.
	     Same trap as the _002E below, one character earlier. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=SuggestVarOrType_005FBuiltInTypes/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=SuggestVarOrType_005FSimpleTypes/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=SuggestVarOrType_005FElsewhere/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=SuggestVarOrType_005FDeconstructionDeclarations/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>

	<!-- A sync call that blocks an async pipeline, made visible.

	     ReSharper reports this one only from inside an async method, so it never fires on
	     deliberately synchronous code — the question it asks is always "you are already async
	     here, why block?". In Core, Roslyn, Xunit and the CLI that question has one answer, and
	     the tree gives it: those projects are clean, so the promotion holds a line already held.

	     The test project is the exception and carries its own layer file turning this off, for the
	     reason written there: its findings are all fixture file IO in arrange blocks. That is a
	     scope, not a demotion — the guard stays live everywhere else, which is the whole point of
	     putting it in the project layer rather than weakening this entry.

	     Inspect-only, like every other severity in this file: cleanup overrides the severity of
	     each rule its enabled modules need, so nothing set here reaches a cleanup task. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=MethodHasAsyncOverload/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>

	<!-- Findings this solution can decide for itself, promoted so they stay decided.

	     Each of these was carried at Suggestion until its sites were cleared, and each is decidable
	     from evidence inside the tree rather than from consumers outside it: a redundant type
	     argument inside nameof, a condition the nullable annotations already answer, a dictionary
	     lookup with a shorter exact equivalent, a gate object that can name its own type now that
	     System.Threading.Lock exists, a member or class whose entire scope is one file. None of them
	     needs weighing per site, so a new one is drift rather than a judgement call, and Warning is
	     the level the baseline actually reads.

	     Where a site is deliberate anyway the house idiom still applies — a ReSharper disable
	     carrying its reason — which is what the ones in FragmentExtractor, TypeNameSimpleTests and
	     AdapterTests do.

	     Two are deliberately NOT promoted beside them. RedundantVerbatimStringPrefix, because every
	     site is a regex pattern, where the verbatim prefix is the convention that keeps the next
	     added backslash from silently meaning something else. And FieldCanBeMadeReadOnly.Global,
	     which would tax every deliberately mutable public field with a disable comment and buy little
	     for it. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=RedundantTypeArgumentsInsideNameof/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ConditionIsAlwaysTrueOrFalseAccordingToNullableAPIContract/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=CanSimplifyDictionaryTryGetValueWithGetValueOrDefault/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=CanSimplifyDictionaryLookupWithTryAdd/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ChangeFieldTypeToSystemThreadingLock/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=MemberCanBePrivate_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ClassNeverInstantiated_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>

	<!-- The rules whose answer this solution does not contain, held at Suggestion on purpose.

	     ReSharper encodes the distinction in the rule ID itself. A .Local finding is decided by
	     the file it sits in: the scope is closed, the evidence complete. A .Global finding is
	     decided by solution-wide search, and this solution ships four public packages, so the
	     consumers that would answer it are outside the search. All seven report the same shape
	     here: a public member nothing in the tree happens to call. Fifteen of those are ITypeInfo
	     and IMemberInfo properties, which are the extension-point contracts the library exists to
	     be implemented against, and which grow additively by design.

	     Suggestion is already their inherited default, so these entries change no behaviour. That
	     is the point. They turn "nobody promoted these" from an absence into a decision with a
	     reason attached, and the pinning test makes a later drive-by promotion or DO_NOT_SHOW a
	     visible edit rather than a silent one. The rule the next person needs: promote what is
	     decidable from evidence this solution contains, declare what is not.

	     Not DO_NOT_SHOW, because the same rules over the test project do find real dead code, and
	     the IDE is where they earn their keep, one member at a time with the context to judge.
	     Where a site is deliberate and worth saying so, the house idiom is a file-scoped ReSharper
	     disable carrying a one-line reason.

	     A dot inside a rule ID escapes as _002E, which is why these keys read oddly. A literal dot
	     produces a key ReSharper never reads: no error, no effect, and a false conclusion that the
	     entry did not work. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=MemberCanBePrivate_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedType_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedMember_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedMethodReturnValue_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedMemberInSuper_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedParameter_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ClassNeverInstantiated_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>

	<!-- Suggestions weighed and declined, each for its own reason rather than by neglect.

	     These are not noise and not defects: they are rewrites that would cost something real here.
	     Recording them at their inherited Suggestion keeps them visible in the IDE, where a human has
	     the context to judge one site, while saying the tree-wide answer has already been thought
	     about.

	     The reasons below name shapes, never site counts. A count is a fact about the tree on the day
	     it was written, and it goes stale the next time anyone adds a loop — after which the entry
	     reads as a decision nobody has revisited, or worse states something that is no longer true.
	     Three of these had drifted before anyone checked, one of them into a sentence that had become
	     simply false.

	     ForCanBeConvertedToForeach — index walks on paths that run once per type in the universe. The
	     checking evaluators are most of them, and SelectionEvaluator says why in prose above one:
	     others sit inside returned closures called per type and per member. TypeNode.IsDeclaredBy is
	     the same shape outside them — the membership predicate behind every arch.Project selection,
	     whose own remarks say why it walks rather than caches.
	     ConvertToLocalFunction and ConvertToPrimaryConstructor — both move where a capture happens,
	     and one of the primary-constructor sites is the memoizing ExtractState whose hiding of an
	     outer static is itself intended.
	     ReplaceWithFieldKeyword and the two IntroduceOptionalParameters — the first changes a readonly
	     field into a property with a backing field, the others change the shape of an overload pair.
	     Neither is a formatting choice.
	     UseObjectOrCollectionInitializer — folding the first Add into an initializer while later ones
	     stay behind reads worse than leaving all of them alone.
	     UseCollectionExpression — every site is the terminal materializer of a multi-line LINQ
	     pipeline, and the spread form moves the opening bracket in front of a chain that reads top to
	     bottom, closing it several lines further down. The pipeline register pinned below (one call
	     per line, leading dot) is what makes those chains legible. Where the source is a single
	     expression the collection expression is already the house form, and that is the case it fits.
	     UseWithExpressionToCopyTuple — the site projects a named tuple into a new one, changing one
	     slot. Spelling both slots out is what shows which is carried through and which is recomputed;
	     a with expression puts that answer inside the copy.
	     RedundantVerbatimStringPrefix — every site is a regex pattern, where the prefix is the
	     convention that stops the next added backslash meaning something else.

	     ConvertToStaticClass is decided too, but at its sites rather than here: each is a nested
	     fixture shape whose nesting is the subject under test, and one is held in lockstep with a
	     source string declaring it the same way, so the house disable-with-reason idiom says so where
	     a reader meets the code. -->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ForCanBeConvertedToForeach/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ConvertToLocalFunction/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ConvertToPrimaryConstructor/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ReplaceWithFieldKeyword/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=IntroduceOptionalParameters_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=IntroduceOptionalParameters_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UseObjectOrCollectionInitializer/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UseCollectionExpression/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UseWithExpressionToCopyTuple/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=RedundantVerbatimStringPrefix/@EntryIndexedValue">SUGGESTION</s:String>

	<!-- Line wrapping is deliberately OFF, and this key is what pins it.

	     Chains here carry two registers. Pipeline chains (LINQ, Shouldly, any sequence of steps)
	     chop one call per line, leading dot (see McpResultAssertions.cs); the test project got
	     that shape in one machine pass, under a temporary WRAP_CHAINED_METHOD_CALLS=CHOP_ALWAYS
	     key removed in the same commit that landed the reformat. Arch-spec chains read as English
	     sentences and are NOT chopped: the statement head and each stage call (.Enforce, .Because,
	     .Fix and the rest) sit one per line, and the subject/verb run inside a clause stays inline
	     while the line fits 120 columns (see CanonicalSampleSpec.cs, GRAMMAR section 12). The
	     formatter's standing job is to PRESERVE both shapes (keep existing line breaks), never to
	     compute either, and the key above is what holds wrapping to that job.

	     Two places were held out of the chop pass and are why no wrapping key may stand here:
	     SpecValidationSpecs.cs, whose forty expected messages in SpecValidationTests.cs quote its
	     anchors as literal "SpecValidationSpecs.cs:N" text, and the Fixtures and Stubs trees, whose
	     goldens pin their line numbers. A chain-wrap key left in place chops them on the next
	     cleanup of any file, silently. Margin wrapping is the same hazard from the other side: it
	     fully chops any chain that already carries one break. Wrapping off is what keeps "any file
	     can be passed to cleanup" true. Shape new chains by hand in their register; cleanup will
	     keep them. -->
	<s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/CodeFormatting/CSharpFormat/WRAP_LINES/@EntryValue">False</s:Boolean>
	<s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/Environment/SettingsMigration/IsMigratorApplied/=JetBrains_002EReSharper_002EPsi_002ECSharp_002ECodeStyle_002ECSharpKeepExistingMigration/@EntryIndexedValue">True</s:Boolean>
	<s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/Environment/SettingsMigration/IsMigratorApplied/=JetBrains_002EReSharper_002EPsi_002ECSharp_002ECodeStyle_002ECSharpPlaceEmbeddedOnSameLineMigration/@EntryIndexedValue">True</s:Boolean>
	<s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/Environment/SettingsMigration/IsMigratorApplied/=JetBrains_002EReSharper_002EPsi_002ECSharp_002ECodeStyle_002ECSharpUseContinuousIndentInsideBracesMigration/@EntryIndexedValue">True</s:Boolean>
	<s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/Environment/SettingsMigration/IsMigratorApplied/=JetBrains_002EReSharper_002EPsi_002ECSharp_002ECodeStyle_002ESettingsUpgrade_002EMigrateBlankLinesAroundFieldToBlankLinesAroundProperty/@EntryIndexedValue">True</s:Boolean>
</wpf:ResourceDictionary>
